\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

% - FB's manipulation failures are associated with systematic errors in predicted future-state occupancy that obscure the relative value of actions needed for task success.
% - Control bottlenecks amplify FB's successor-estimation errors: these errors are larger around interactions where a restricted set of actions determines subsequent task progress.
% - The normalized parameterization and categorical Bellman objective used by \name{} reduce task-relevant successor-estimation errors and improve action rankings, particularly around the interactions identified in Section~\ref{sec:exp-bottlenecks}.
% - The improvements in successor estimation translate into higher task performance, with larger gains on tasks requiring the interactions for which prediction accuracy improves.
% - The learned successor representation supports useful policy selection for reward functions withheld during pretraining, including objectives that require different preferences over future states.

Our experiments test each step of the argument developed in Sections~\ref{sec:failure} and~\ref{sec:method}.
We organize them around four questions.
\begin{inparaenum}[(i)]
    \item  does standard successor regression lose the action-relevant information identified in Section~\ref{sec:failure}? Section~\ref{sec:exp-didactic} isolates this question in the controlled bottleneck MDP, where the exact successor measure and the correct action values are known.
    \item does this difference in successor learning matter for actual control? Section~\ref{sec:exp-ogbench} tests whether replacing the standard FB objective with \name{} improves performance on OGBench manipulation tasks, while keeping the dataset, representation, actor, and training procedure fixed.
    \item is the effect specific to one benchmark or one successor-learning method? Section~\ref{sec:exp-generality} evaluates the same change across additional successor-based methods and manipulation environments with different dynamics and control interfaces.
    \item Can \name{} transfer to new rewards during test time ? Section~\ref{sec:reward-transfer} evaluates frozen representations on rewards introduced only after training.
\end{inparaenum}

\paragraph{Experimental protocol.}
Unless stated otherwise, all methods use state observations and are trained for $10^6$ gradient steps on the same fixed offline dataset.
We report results over five independent training seeds.
For matched comparisons, we keep the network architecture, task sampling, actor, training budget, and evaluation procedure fixed, and change only the successor-learning objective.
For offline policy learning, we use the flow-based behavior-cloning actor from \citet{bagatella-iclr26a}, based on Flow Q-Learning~\citep{park-icml25a}.
The flow model estimates the behavior policy, while the learned actor is regularized toward actions sampled from this model.
Our primary zero-shot comparisons infer the task index from reward samples for all methods.
When a method additionally supports direct goal conditioning, we report that route separately.
Full architectures, hyperparameters, and evaluation details are given in \aditya{Add Appendix}.

\subsection{Does normalized successor learning preserve the bottleneck decision?}
\label{sec:exp-didactic}

\paragraph{Hypothesis.}
Section~\ref{sec:failure} predicts that squared successor regression can fit most of the successor measure while losing a smaller difference that determines which action is preferred.
If this is the failure addressed by \name{}, the normalized objective should preserve this action difference at substantially lower representation rank.

We test this prediction on the nine-state process from Section~\ref{sec:failure}.
This experiment isolates successor estimation from policy learning: both critics use the same fixed uniform continuation policy, the same transitions, exact expectations, and no learned actor.
We vary the rank of the forward--backward factorization and compare the action values recovered from standard FB and \name{}.

At rank $3$, the exact successor measure assigns values $2.20$ and $0.80$ to the two actions at state $7$.
FB predicts $1.04$ and $1.01$, making the two actions nearly indistinguishable.
\name{} predicts $2.00$ and $0.62$, preserving both the correct ordering and most of the action-value difference.

The rank sweep shows that this is not specific to one representation size.
FB preserves only $1\%$--$2\%$ of the exact action gap at ranks $2$ and $3$, $34\%$ at rank $4$, and requires rank $6$ out of nine states to recover $99\%$ of the gap.
\name{} preserves $55\%$ of the gap at rank $2$ and at least $99\%$ from rank $3$ onward.
Normalizing the backward features to unit norm does not remove the failure for FB.

\paragraph{Result.}
Standard FB can represent most of the successor structure while losing the difference required to choose the correct action.
Learning the normalized successor distribution preserves this difference with substantially lower rank, matching the mechanism predicted in Section~\ref{sec:failure}.

\begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.56\textwidth]{ICLR/figures/didactic/fig_didactic_values.pdf}
    \caption{
    \textbf{Normalized successor learning preserves the bottleneck decision.}
    The two actions at state $7$ have clearly different values under the exact successor measure.
    FB makes them nearly indistinguishable at rank $3$, whereas \name{} preserves the difference.
    Error bars show variation across training runs.
    }
    \label{fig:didactic-values}
\end{figure}


\subsection{Does the change in successor learning improve manipulation control?}
\label{sec:exp-ogbench}

\paragraph{Hypothesis.}
The controlled experiment shows that \name{} better preserves an action-relevant successor difference.
If the same failure contributes to the difficulty of successor methods on manipulation, this change should improve downstream control when task success depends on precise interactions with an object.

We test this on the OGBench manipulation domains \texttt{cube-single}, \texttt{cube-double}, and \texttt{scene}.
These environments provide a useful progression in task complexity, from moving a single object to tasks requiring multiple interactions and coordinated manipulation.
For this comparison, FB and \name{} use the same dataset, representation size, actor, task-sampling procedure, and reward-inference procedure.
The only change is the objective used to learn the successor representation.

Table~\ref{tab:exp-ogbench-density} reports the resulting success rates.
In the current runs, \name{} improves over FB from $45.6$ to $81.3$ on \texttt{cube-single}, from $2.2$ to $30.4$ on \texttt{cube-double}, and from $10.2$ to $52.7$ on \texttt{scene}.
The improvement is therefore not limited to the simplest reaching or pushing behavior and remains large as manipulation requires more precise sequences of interactions.

\paragraph{Result.}
The change in successor estimation translates into substantially better closed-loop control on all three OGBench manipulation domains.
Together with the controlled experiment, this shows that the effect is not only a reduction in prediction error: preserving the relevant successor differences changes the learned policy enough to improve task success.

