\subsection{Does the effect extend beyond one method and benchmark?}
\label{sec:exp-generality}

\paragraph{Hypothesis.}
The change introduced by \name{} concerns how a successor quantity is learned, rather than an FB-specific architectural component.
If the explanation in Section~\ref{sec:failure} is correct, the same normalized objective should be useful when applied to other successor-based representations and in manipulation environments outside OGBench.

We test these two forms of generality separately.
First, we replace the original successor-learning objective in FB, HILP, and RLDP while leaving the remainder of each method unchanged.
\aditya{Insert cross-backbone results here.}
This comparison tests whether the effect depends on a particular FB implementation or instead follows from the successor-learning objective itself.

Second, we evaluate FB and \name{} on independently collected MetaWorld and ManiSkill datasets.
These environments differ from OGBench in their state spaces, dynamics, and control interfaces.
They nevertheless contain the pattern that motivates our analysis: after a successful interaction, the manipulated object often remains in approximately the same state for many subsequent transitions.
Across these datasets, the fraction of such transitions ranges from $0.76$ to $0.95$.

Table~\ref{tab:exp-extended-density} reports the manipulation results.
In the current experiments, \name{} improves over FB on all eight tasks.
The largest gains occur on tasks such as bin picking, cube picking, and peg lifting, where progress depends on first establishing a successful interaction with the object and then maintaining it during subsequent motion.

\paragraph{Result.}
The benefit is not specific to the OGBench cube environments.
It persists across manipulation suites with different dynamics and control interfaces.
