\subsection{Does the learned representation remain useful for new rewards?}
\label{sec:reward-transfer}

\paragraph{Hypothesis.}
A successor representation is useful because it separates learning the consequences of behavior from specifying which consequences are desirable.
Changing the estimation objective should therefore improve successor prediction without sacrificing the ability to reuse the learned representation when the downstream reward changes.

We test this by specifying rewards only after pretraining.
All representation and policy parameters remain frozen.
For each new reward, we evaluate the reward on states from the offline dataset, infer the corresponding task index, and execute the resulting policy without additional gradient updates or environment interaction.

We evaluate \aditya{state the exact reward families here: e.g., held-out goal rewards, sparse rewards, and dense shaped rewards}.
These rewards differ from those used to construct the task distribution during pretraining and therefore test whether the learned successor representation can be reused rather than only reproducing its training tasks.

We compare two evaluation routes on the same frozen checkpoints.
The reward-inference route constructs the task index using only reward samples and is our primary zero-shot setting.
Where available, we additionally report direct goal conditioning.
The latter removes task inference from the comparison and therefore provides a diagnostic of how much remaining error comes from selecting the task index rather than from the learned successor representation.

\paragraph{Result.}
\aditya{Insert result once the final withheld-reward table is available.}
The important comparison here is not only whether \name{} obtains high absolute return, but whether the same frozen successor model remains effective as the reward changes.
This distinguishes improved successor learning from specialization to the manipulation rewards used during pretraining.